    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_0066a0b257ffd6d4713fbfa57744fd0f566682aba677d979f85019e5354ba88f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Harmonic and geometric families reach comparable local and Sato–Tate measures through different averaging engines. In the harmonic route, the identity orbital term produces unramified Plancherel mass and the L-map pushes it to Satake statistics; in the geometric route, parameter counting first descends modulo p to an atomic Frobenius measure, and a lisse sheaf's monodromy turns that measure into a Haar pushforward. The common output is a family measure, but the representations compressed and the error burdens carried by the two routes remain different.",
