    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_27d0638e81901706ac45f079629accdc2e7d050c665a5d48446929edaa7de3af",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A statistically usable family needs two independent resources: a controlled counting measure on its parameters and a positive-mass mechanism for isolating its spectral conditions. Finite-to-one parametrization, or explicit substitute constraints, makes conductor balls finite and local coefficient averages meaningful; unrestricted Diophantine bases or intersections can destroy those averages. Positive Plancherel volume supplies the trace-formula isolation mechanism, whereas a Plancherel-null condition removes that ordinary counting resource. These tractability conditions delimit where the proposed ensemble statistics can be formed, without deciding whether the individual L-functions are mathematically natural.",
