    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_526528faf35f563e548836f856805d0fba503baf7aca7d5215719182cf8785bf",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The family measure is first compressed by the three indicators into unitary, orthogonal, and symplectic proportions; low-zero symmetry then requires two independent refinements before the universality table applies: remove persistent central zeros measured by the next-order rank statistic, and split the symplectic mass by the ramified root number. Only after those steps does conductor scaling send rank-zero homogeneous pieces to U, Sp, SOeven, or SOodd statistics, so the random-matrix class is obtained by a composed inference rather than by reading a group name.",
